# The TLS terminator.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHY THIS EXISTS AT ALL
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Production publishes the edge to loopback and deliberately left HTTPS as a
# deployment decision. Until something terminated it, secure WebSockets did not
# work, the block-token cookie could not be `Secure`, and every message crossed
# the network in clear text.
#
# Caddy, because certificates are the whole job and it does them without a
# second moving part: it obtains and renews from Let's Encrypt on its own, with
# no cron entry, no renewal hook and no certificate path to get wrong. On a
# single host that is the entire argument.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TWO PROXIES NOW SIT IN FRONT OF THE BACKEND
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# This is the setting that breaks everything quietly if it is wrong.
#
# The chain is client -> Caddy -> nginx -> backend, and the backend counts
# `X-Forwarded-For` entries from the right: the rightmost N were written by our
# own infrastructure and anything left of them is whatever the client sent.
#
# What the backend actually receives, confirmed by running this chain and
# reading the header at the far end rather than by reasoning about it:
#
#   real client        added by nginx
#   198.51.100.7   ,   172.20.0.4
#   ^ TRUST_PROXY_HOPS=2 lands here
#
# Two entries, not three, and this is the part worth knowing: **Caddy discards
# a client-supplied `X-Forwarded-For` rather than appending to it.** Requests
# arriving with `X-Forwarded-For: 1.2.3.4` reach the backend with that value
# gone entirely. Caddy only preserves an inbound chain when the peer is listed
# in `trusted_proxies`, and nothing is. That is a stricter default than nginx's
# `$proxy_add_x_forwarded_for`, and it means address forgery is not possible
# here at any hop count.
#
# The hop count still has to be right, because it decides which entry is read.
# Set it to 1 and the backend reads nginx's own address for every user, so the
# entire service shares one rate-limit bucket and one block identity — every
# ban applies to everybody, and every stranger looks like the same person.
# `docker-compose.prod.yml` therefore requires the value explicitly rather than
# defaulting it.
#
# Putting a CDN in front — Cloudflare, most likely — adds a hop and makes it 3,
# and Cloudflare must then be in `trusted_proxies` here for the real client
# address to survive at all. That is the change to make, and forgetting it is
# the trap.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NO COMPRESSION
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Deliberately absent. Security §17 says not to enable compression for a small
# text-only service without testing that shows a benefit, and while that section
# is about the WebSocket rather than static assets, the principle it states —
# do not enable it simply because it is available — is the one being followed.
# The bundle is small and cached hard by the layer behind this.

{
	# Where Let's Encrypt sends expiry warnings if renewal ever stops working.
	email {$ACME_EMAIL}
}

{$KAROBAKBAK_DOMAIN} {
	# HSTS is set here because this is the only layer that knows the request
	# arrived over TLS. Two years, subdomains included, and deliberately **no**
	# `preload`: preloading is a one-way door enforced by browsers for months
	# regardless of what this file later says, and it should be a considered
	# decision rather than a default picked up from an example config.
	header {
		Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=63072000; includeSubDomains"
		# Caddy announces itself otherwise. Nothing needs to know.
		-Server
	}

	# Everything, including the WebSocket upgrade. Caddy proxies `Upgrade`
	# without configuration, and appends the client address to
	# `X-Forwarded-For` rather than replacing it — which is what makes the hop
	# counting above work.
	#
	# nginx behind this keeps serving the bundle and setting the Content
	# Security Policy; this layer adds TLS and nothing else, so there is one
	# place security headers are decided rather than two that can disagree.
	reverse_proxy frontend:80

	log {
		output stdout
		format console
		# Access logs carry client addresses, which are personal data here and
		# are salted-hashed everywhere else in this system. Errors only.
		level ERROR
	}
}
